module examples.err_string_capacity_build_limit

# `cargo xtask fuzz lower --seed 101`, iteration 6134 (2026-07-26).
#
# plans/M9.md item K1 added `string_capacity_fits` to stop a
# layout-unrepresentable `String[..N]` from typechecking and then panicking
# at lowering. It checked the wrong thing: that `8 * (1 + N)` does not
# overflow `usize`. That admits N = 2^60 — the byte count is perfectly
# computable — and then `lower::emit_string_aggregate` calls
# `Vec::with_capacity(1 + N)` and Rust aborts with `capacity overflow`,
# which is a bare panic, not an `internal error:` diagnostic.
#
# The fuzzer found it by truncating `err-fstring-bound-overflow` so the
# declared `String[..2^60]` survived without the concat sum K1 *did* guard
# — i.e. the guard was real but reachable around.
#
# The fix makes the rule a **build limit** rather than an overflow check,
# reusing the number `[elem; N]` already carries (65536 elements). This
# case pins the declaration site; `err-string-concat-overflow` and
# `err-fstring-bound-overflow` pin the two sum sites, both re-based onto
# individually-legal operands so they still test summation.

fn wide() -> String[..1152921504606846976]:
    return "x"
